  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 AM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\n- Mix of Rain and Snow Showers This Afternoon/Evening\n\nThe upper trough axis pushes over Lake Michigan in the 18z-00z\ntime frame today with lake enhanced precipitation possible\nespecially near and north of I-96 with both rain and snow showers\ninitially, then transitioning to snow showers toward dark. While\nthe DGZ looks to briefly become saturated, lift below the DGZ in a\nrimed layer that is saturated (ie. -5C to -10C) along with low RH\nin the 0-2000ft layer will combine to thwart significant snow\nrates. Still, brief bursts of snow are possible this afternoon and\nevening with the possibility for a few tenths of an inch north of\nI-96 and up to 1 inch along US 10 could lead to localized slick\nspots on roads that sufficiently cool after dark tonight. No\nheadlines are planned for this brief event.\n\n- Rain Favored Thanksgiving into Friday, Mixing with Snow at Times\n\nEnsemble and deterministic guidance is remaining fairly \nconsistent that the western reaches of a developing Mid Atlantic\ncyclone should impact Lower Michigan Thanksgiving into Friday,\nwith mainly rain expected but a period of wet snow is also\npossible. The dominant subtropical jet that has been active with\nthis powerful El Nino will develop a wave across Texas Tuesday\ninto Wednesday, which is likely to phase with a deepening trough\nand eventual upper low across the Midwest Wednesday. This phased\nsystem should propagate across the Midwest and Ohio Valley into\nThanksgiving. The exact positioning of this deepening upper low \nis still a bit uncertain at this range but by 12z Thursday the \n500mb mean heights via the ECE, GEFS, and CMC all show an upper \nlow near St. Louis. This upper low is favored to move across NE \nOhio and western PA by 12z Friday.\n\nECE member low positions Thursday night into Friday reveal a\nfairly big spread in geographic position, with a range of Ohio to\nLong Island all possibilities. Ultimately what may end up\nhappening is a longitudinally broad area of low pressure that may\nbe centered somewhere across PA but with a precipitation shield\nthat extends all the way west to Lake Michigan and northern IL /\nsouthern WI late Thursday into Friday. What this system lacks is\ncold air aloft and at the surface. The DGZ will be sky high at\n15k-20k ft with a 10-15k ft layer of supercooled water below it.\nRain is favored Thanksgiving with some wet and heavily rimed snow\npossible Thanksgiving night into early Friday. NBM low\ntemperatures Thursday night only dip into the low to mid 30s. Any\nsnow that does occur will have little chance of accumulating\nexcept maybe on unpaved surfaces before midday Friday. Cobb Bufkit\noverviews via the GFS indicate 3:1 snow ratios, not exactly\noptimal if you like accumulating snow. Still, a light sloppy\ncoating of wet snow is possible early Friday on unpaved surfaces.\n\nAs for rainfall amounts, this is looking like a solid 0.25\"-0.75\"\nrainfall for the region, highest amounts along and south of I-96.\nNotably, you don't have to go far up the ensemble envelope to\nreach higher amounts, such as the ECE 75th percentile rainfall\nindicating 1.00\"-1.25\" for places like Grand Rapids, Lansing, \nKalamazoo, and Jackson. So, there is certainly potential for this \nto be more of a soaker event for the region.\n\n- Chance for Weekend Rain Showers, Maybe Mixed with Snow\n\nRenewed upper troughing over the Great Lakes this weekend looks to\nhelp produce occasional lake enhanced precipitation with W to SW\nlow level flow over Lake Michigan. Rain showers may be mixed with\nsome wet snow at times.",
